{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791618467,"nonce":"z+EVXsMncK56QByBvJTKuc5bn5s=","title":"Every pass that reads dominance builds its own dominator tree","message":"cse, branch_fold, licm, error_flag, bounds_version, drop_insert, loop_specialize, the loop and vectorisation passes and pure_call_pre each call DominatorTree::new on the function they rewrite, so one optimisation of method_call's lua$chunk builds about 25 trees, most of them over a CFG the previous pass left as it was. DominatorTree::new was 6.7% of run_interp_safe_opts_with in a repeated start-up profile of method_call.\n\nFix: DominatorTree::of keeps, per thread, the last tree it built with an exact key of what a tree is built from (the entry, the blocks in order, their successor and predecessor lists) and hands it out again while the key matches; the passes call it instead of new.\n\nChecked: optimised bodies of nbody, method_call, op_overload, binary_trees, mandelbrot, collatz, tokenize, records and pipeline with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 are identical once ids are masked. Start-up instructions (llvm-backend build, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3, Mac): Lua 17 benchmarks 25.10G -\u003e 24.66G (-1.7%; method_call -2.8%, pipeline, records and tokenize about -2.5%); Python 9 kernels -0.1%.","files":null}]}